# NHANES exploratory data analysis

This is a bounded one-to-one port of the descriptive-statistics and exploratory-data-analysis sequence in [*Biological Data Science with R*, Essential statistics](https://bdsr.stephenturner.us/stats.html). Select **Prepare all** first: Studio downloads the two exact source-hosted files and verifies their sizes and SHA-256 checksums.

## 8.1 Our data: NHANES

### 8.1.1 About NHANES

NHANES combines interviews, examinations, and laboratory measurements. The chapter uses a 5,000-person resampled teaching extract; reproduce the chapter with this table, but use the original survey design and weights for population inference.

### 8.1.2 Import and inspect

```biolang
let nh = read_csv("nhanes.csv")
let dictionary = read_csv("nhanes_dd.csv")
{people: nrow(nh), variables: ncol(nh), dictionary_rows: nrow(dictionary)}
```

The source uses the 5,000-person, 32-variable resampled NHANES teaching table. It is appropriate for reproducing the lesson, but it is not a replacement for survey-weighted analysis of the original NHANES sample.

## 8.2 Descriptive statistics

> **Understanding descriptive statistics**
>
> **Use them when:** you need to describe the observations you actually have before making claims beyond them.
>
> **How to read them:** centre answers where typical values lie, spread answers how different they are, and shape shows asymmetry, tails, gaps, or multiple groups. No single summary replaces the others.

### Reproduce the source exercises

```biolang
let pulse = nh |> drop_null("Pulse") |> col("Pulse")
let diastolic = nh |> drop_null("BPDia") |> col("BPDia")
let age = nh |> col("Age")
let income = nh |> drop_null("Income") |> col("Income")
{
    mean_pulse: mean(pulse),
    diastolic_range: [summary(diastolic).min, summary(diastolic).max],
    age_quartiles: [quantile(age, 0.25), quantile(age, 0.5), quantile(age, 0.75)],
    income_variance: variance(income),
    income_standard_deviation: stdev(income)
}
```

These are the same calculations and the same source table used by the chapter exercises.

### 8.2.1 Missing data

> **Understanding missing values**
>
> **Use this check when:** any variable may be absent, unrecorded, inapplicable, or lost.
>
> **How to read it:** report both the full row count and the number contributing to each result. Missingness can bias a result when the reason a value is absent is related to the quantity being studied.

```biolang
let income_summary = summary(nh |> col("Income"))
{
    total_rows: nrow(nh),
    observed_income: income_summary.count,
    missing_income: nrow(nh) - income_summary.count,
    mean_observed_income: mean(income)
}
```

### 8.2.2 Exploratory data analysis

> **Understanding exploratory data analysis**
>
> **Use it when:** you need to see distributions, groups, unusual values, and relationships before selecting a formal model.
>
> **How to read it:** plots suggest what to investigate and reveal assumptions that numbers can hide. They do not supply a p-value and should not be mined until a preferred story appears.

#### Histograms

The source draws four `geom_histogram()` panels. BioLang regenerates each one from the exact NHANES rows with the same bin counts, grey fill, ggplot panel, and axis labels.

### BMI, 30 bins

```biolang
histogram(nh |> col("BMI"), {
    bins: 30, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "BMI", ylabel: "count", width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

### Weight in kilograms, 30 bins

```biolang
let observed_weight = nh |> drop_null("Weight") |> col("Weight")
histogram(observed_weight, {
    bins: 30, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "Weight", ylabel: "count", width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

### Weight converted to pounds, 80 bins

```biolang
let weight_pounds = observed_weight |> map(|value| value * 2.2)
histogram(weight_pounds, {
    bins: 80, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "Weight * 2.2", ylabel: "count", width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

### Age, 30 bins

```biolang
histogram(age, {
    bins: 30, bin_rule: "ggplot", theme: "ggplot", title: "",
    xlabel: "Age", ylabel: "count", width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
})
```

#### Scatterplots: height and weight

The source first plots all available observations and colours points by gender. BioLang uses the same complete rows and ggplot hue ordering.

```biolang
let everyone_complete = nh |> drop_null("Height") |> drop_null("Weight") |> drop_null("Gender")
stats_relationship_plot(
    everyone_complete |> col("Height"), everyone_complete |> col("Weight"),
    {
        group: everyone_complete |> col("Gender"), fit: false, interval: "none",
        theme: "ggplot", title: "", x_label: "Height", y_label: "Weight",
        legend_title: "Gender", note: false, width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
    }
)
```

It then filters to `Age >= 18` and adds one linear-model smooth per gender with the source's grey confidence ribbons.

```biolang
let adults_complete = nh |> filter(|row| row.Age >= 18) |> drop_null("Height") |> drop_null("Weight") |> drop_null("Gender")
stats_relationship_plot(
    adults_complete |> col("Height"), adults_complete |> col("Weight"),
    {
        group: adults_complete |> col("Gender"), interval: "confidence", confidence: 0.95,
        theme: "ggplot", title: "", x_label: "Height", y_label: "Weight",
        legend_title: "Gender", note: false, width: 840, height: 600, format: "svg"
    }
)
```

```biolang
{
    complete_height_weight_gender: nrow(everyone_complete),
    complete_adult_height_weight_gender: nrow(adults_complete)
}
```

## Fidelity checks

```biolang
assert nrow(nh) == 5000, "exact source row count"
assert ncol(nh) == 32, "exact source column count"
assert abs(mean(pulse) - 73.63382) < 0.00001, "source pulse mean"
assert summary(diastolic).min == 0, "source minimum diastolic pressure"
assert summary(diastolic).max == 116, "source maximum diastolic pressure"
assert quantile(age, 0.25) == 17, "source lower age quartile"
assert quantile(age, 0.5) == 36, "source median age"
assert quantile(age, 0.75) == 54, "source upper age quartile"
assert nrow(everyone_complete) == 4834, "source complete-case count"
assert nrow(adults_complete) == 3676, "source adult complete-case count"
"Exact source data and runtime-figure checks passed."
```
